{"text":"MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "Chronicle and Hall & Oates feel like natural next choices because I have been choosing familiar older rock and melodic pop, often through hits collections.",
    "Glen Campbell appeals to the softer, melodic side of my interest in Jonathan Edwards and mid-'60s songs, while ABBA fits my interest in catchy pop collections."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "Roger Miller and Merle Haggard lean toward country in a way that does not follow what I currently want from older rock, folk-pop, and pop songs.",
    "Sheryl Crow and Nick Of Time have a weaker connection to the older, familiar hits I want now; being broadly related to rock or pop does not make them equally appealing."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "A greatest-hits title alone seems to count too much: the artist and kind of songs matter more than the compilation format.",
    "Older music seems to be treated as one broad preference, although my interest in older melodic rock and folk-pop does not extend equally to traditional country."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "I already interacted with a Simon & Garfunkel compilation, so another one can match my taste closely without being something I would choose now.",
    "Familiarity affects which appealing collection I choose first: Atlanta Rhythm Section interests me, but Chronicle and Hall & Oates feel more immediately compelling."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "The recommendations recognize my interest in familiar older hits but stretch that preference too far across musical styles and overlook overlap with music I have already explored."
}"}
